      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Hyperventilation symptoms are among the sensations asthma patients tend to report during exacerbations of their disease. However, little is known about their importance for the patients' perceived well-being."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHOD",
          "content": "We therefore studied the association of reported hyperventilation symptoms (Asthma Symptom Checklist) with perceived physical and mental health (36-item Short-form Health Survey) in a sample of primary care asthma patients (N = 190). We also studied the potential role of perceived control of asthma (Perceived Control of Asthma Questionnaire) in mediating any association between these variables."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In hierarchical multiple regression analyses controlling for demographics, asthma severity, and other asthma symptoms (obstruction, congestion, panic-fear, irritation, and fatigue), hyperventilation symptoms were significantly associated with both physical and mental health status. This association was partially mediated by perceived control of asthma."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "It is possible that hypocapnia creates symptoms that asthma patients cannot control by using their antiasthmatic medication, thus compromising their perceived control over the management of their asthma, and consequently their perceived health. Behavioral interventions should address the problem of hyperventilation in asthma."
        }
      ]},
